7\nL274:  |  |  |  |  | E2E correctness failure  | 1  | 0  | 4  | 5\nL275: Total  | 36  | 23  | 11  | 70  | Total  | 16  | 34  | 35  | 85\nL276: Table 1: Comprehensive KernelBench results vs. torch.compile. Panel A: Level summary; Matched includes both correct kernels within the 3% noise margin and cases where no optimization was attempted (deployed model is the compiler baseline). Panel B: Speedup distribution over the 70 optimized kernels. Panel C: Dominant operation breakdown (by the highest-speedup sub-kernel per problem; name-based classification).\nL277: Panel D: Fallback root causes, classified by dominant Triton kernel name among the 73 performance-regression fallbacks (cuBLAS/cuDNN dominance accounts for 75%), plus 7 no-Triton-kernel and 5 terminal-correctness-failure cases.\nL278: #### Speedup analysis.\nL279: \nL280: The speedup distribution is heavy-tailed (Panel B). Table cite109†8 (Appendix cite50†E ) illustrates how NCU bottleneck metrics (compute, memory, occupancy) drive the planner’s strategy selection across all three levels. The largest gains arise from two mechanisms.\nL281: Algorithmic optimization and fusion. The top five speedups span both mechanisms: L1 012 (diagonal matmul, $O(N^{3})\\to O(N^{2})$ row-scaling, 88.63$\\times$), L2 018 (operator fusion and algebraic simplification of a 5-operation chain, 33.11$\\times$), L1 015 (lower-triangular matmul, skip-zero iteration, 20.26$\\times$), L1 014 (upper-triangular solve, 14.04$\\times$), and L1 085 (batched diagonal, 11.38$\\times$).\nL282: Operator fusion. On L2 064, four fused pointwise operations on a $(256,4096)$ tensor eliminate intermediate memory round-trips (8.30$\\times$). L2 022 achieves 7.18$\\times$ via similar epilogue fusion.\nL283: \nL284: Beyond the 250 KernelBench problems, KernelOPT also improves 4/10 official Triton tutorial kernels (best $1.70\\times$) and 5/10 Helion tutorials (best $1.08\\times$); see Appendix cite66†H .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKernelOPT: Dispatch-Aware Agentic Searchfor GPU Kernel Optimization (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30059v2)\nciteturn84view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30059v2\",\"pattern\":\"beam\"}); Total lines: 1426\nL163: 6:   $n_{c}\\leftarrow|\\mathcal{B}_{t-1}|$;  $N_{c}\\leftarrow\\max(1,\\lfloor N/n_{c}\\rfloor)$ {total plan budget $N$ is divided across the $n_{c}$ active chains, not issued per chain}\nL164: \nL165: 7:   for $(k_{i},\\tau_{i},\\pi_{i},b_{i},h_{i})\\in\\mathcal{B}_{t-1}$ do\nL166: \nL167: 8:    $\\{p_{1},\\ldots,p_{N_{c}}\\}\\leftarrow\\text{Planner}_{\\theta}(\\pi_{i},b_{i},k_{i},\\mathcal{M},h_{i})$\nL168: \nL169: 9:    for $j=1$ to $N_{c}$ do\nL170: \nL171: 10:     $k^{\\prime}\\leftarrow\\text{Executor}_{\\theta}(k_{i},p_{j},K)$\nL172: 11:     $\\mathcal{M}\\leftarrow\\text{Summarizer}_{\\theta}(k_{i},k^{\\prime},p_{j},\\mathcal{M})$\nL173: \nL174: 12:     $c^{\\prime}\\leftarrow V_{\\text{stat}}(k^{\\prime})\\wedge V_{\\text{corr}}(k^{\\prime},k_{0})$ {Gates 1–2 status, recorded but not yet enforced}\nL175: \nL176: 13:     $\\pi^{\\prime}\\leftarrow\\text{Profiler}(k^{\\prime})$;  $b^{\\prime}\\leftarrow\\text{Analyst}(\\pi^{\\prime})$\nL177: \nL178: 14:     $\\mathcal{C}_{t}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{C}_{t}\\cup\\{(k^{\\prime},\\tau(k^{\\prime}),\\pi^{\\prime},b^{\\prime},h_{i}\\cup\\{p_{j}\\},c^{\\prime})\\}$\nL179: 15:    end for\nL180: \nL181: 16:   end for\nL182: \nL183: 17:   $\\mathcal{B}_{t}\\leftarrow\\text{DiverseSelect}(\\mathcal{C}_{t},B)$ {ranks by $\\tau(\\cdot)$; does not filter on $c^{\\prime}$ (§cite18†4.3 )}\nL184: \nL185: 18:   if no improvement for 2 consecutive iterations then\nL186: \nL187: 19:    break\nL188: \nL189: 20:   end if\nL190: \nL191: 21:  end for\nL192: \nL193: 22:  $k_{\\text{fast}}\\leftarrow\\arg\\min_{(k,\\tau,\\cdot,\\cdot,\\cdot,\\cdot)\\in\\bigcup_{t}\\mathcal{B}_{t}}\\tau$ {lowest-latency candidate, irrespective of $c^{\\prime}$}\nL194: 23:  return $k_{\\text{fast}}$ if $\\mathcal{V}(k_{\\text{fast}},k_{0})=1$ (Def. cite101†2 ), else $k_{0}$ {single candidate tested; no retry over lower-ranked candidates}\nL195: #### Beam Dynamics.\nL196: The algorithm maintains beam width $B$ via the DiverseSelect call that closes each iteration. At iteration $t=0$, $\\mathcal{B}_{0}=\\{k_{0}\\}$ (single baseline).\nL197: Rather than issuing $N$ plans per beam element, the Planner divides a fixed per-iteration plan budget $N$ across the $n_{c}=|\\mathcal{B}_{t-1}|$ active chains ($N_{c}=\\max(1,\\lfloor N/n_{c}\\rfloor)$ plans per chain), so $\\mathcal{C}_{t}$ receives at most $N$ candidates per iteration regardless of $B$ (consistent with the $T{=}5$, $N{=}4$ configuration in §cite24†5 yielding 20 total candidates per problem, not $T\\cdot N\\cdot B$).\nL198: DiverseSelect partitions $\\mathcal{C}_{t}$ by chain origin and returns at most $B$ candidates (best per chain by latency + global best), so widening $B$ trades search depth within a chain for breadth across chains at fixed total plan budget, rather than increasing the total number of candidates evaluated.\nL199: #### Implementation notes on final selection.\nL200: Two aspects of the released implementation differ from an idealized reading of Eq. cite102†1 : mid-search ranking is latency-first (the Gates 1–2 correctness flag $c^{\\prime}$ is recorded but not enforced before ranking), and final selection tests only the single fastest candidate $k_{\\text{fast}}$ against Gates 3–4, with no retry over lower-ranked candidates if it fails.\nL201: Neither compromises the non-regression guarantee in Table cite83†1 – Gates 3–4 are mandatory and unconditional on the final model, and fire throughout our evaluation (167 rejections across 46 problems, Appendix cite67†I ) – but both affect search efficiency and completeness; see Appendix cite42†B for the full discussion.\nL202: The diversity-aware selection (the DiverseSelect call in Algorithm cite103†1 ) partitions the candidate set $\\mathcal{C}$ by chain origin $\\mathcal{C}_{i}$ and proceeds in two phases:\nL203: \nL204:  | $$\\text{DiverseSelect}(\\mathcal{C},B)=\\underbrace{\\{\\arg\\min_{c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{i}}\\tau(c)\\}_{i\\in\\text{chains}}}_{\\text{best per chain}}\\;\\cup\\;\\underbrace{\\text{top-}(B-|\\text{chains}|)}_{\\text{global best}}$$  |  | (3)\nL205: Phase 1 ensures each chain contributes its best candidate (exploration); Phase 2 fills remaining beam slots by global latency (exploitation).\nL206: ### 4.4 Verification Cascade\nL207: \nL208: The four gates are applied sequentially: Gates 1–2 filter every candidate during optimization; Gates 3–4 verify the re-stitched model end-to-end.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKREX: Concurrent Kernel Benchmarking on Shared GPUs via Region-Granular Exclusivity (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30057v1)\nciteturn84view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30057v1\",\"pattern\":\"AMD\"}); Total lines: 331\nL110: R4 (heterogeneity). The same region interface and isolation contract must hold on diverse hardware, specifically NVIDIA and AMD GPUs, even when their enforcement mechanisms differ.\nL119: R3 (throughput): Off-region concurrency overlaps commands outside regions, while the persistent context pool pays context creation costs at startup or recovery rather than on every command (§cite15†4.3 ). R4 (heterogeneity): Vendor-specific adaptations implement the shared region protocol on NVIDIA and AMD (§cite16†5 ).\nL137: We describe the GPU protocol in terms of these context processes; on AMD, a runtime-loaded hook executes the same protocol directly in the client process (§cite16†5 ).\nL175: If a candidate completes normally and cleanup finishes without errors, the context process will be reused to serve the next candidate. Therefore, a candidate failure is confined only to that run and can be recovered by a simple context-process respawn.\nL176: ## 5. Implementation\nL177: \nL178: KREX supports NVIDIA and AMD GPUs with about 20,000 lines of C++ for GPU-call interception and dispatch. The dispatcher, per-GPU coordinators, and kernel-evaluation service use 4,800 lines of Python outside the GPU-call path. Another 41,000 lines of C++ CUDA and NVML forwarding stubs are machine generated from annotated headers to avoid hand-written marshalling for new entry points.\nL179: The NVIDIA and AMD implementations follow the same region protocol in §cite14†4.2 but differ in interception, draining, and freezing. On NVIDIA, the gate is implemented in the context process’s RPC dispatch loop. On AMD, the gate can be implemented either in the same pooled path or in a tools library loaded by ROCm at startup inside the tenant process. The library patches the runtime dispatch table to intercept each queue’s kernel launches.\nL180: KREX provides both implementations and exposes them as configuration options. Unlike NVIDIA’s host-submission counter, AMD’s counter tracks device completion: the counter is incremented on entry to each operation and decremented by a polling thread when the operation’s completion signal fires. The holder waits for sibling counters to reach zero without sibling-side context synchronization or dedicated drainer threads.\nL181: On AMD, tenants are added to freezer cgroups only after initialization because freezing during initial memory registration causes failures on the evaluated system.\nL182: ## 6. Evaluation\nL183: Our evaluation answers five questions: (Q1) how long benchmarking commands run and what fraction of their execution time is spent in critical regions (§cite19†6.2 ); (Q2) how much throughput KREX gains over command-granular exclusivity (§cite20†6.3 ); (Q3) how closely its timings and candidate rankings match uncontended execution (§cite21†6.4 ); (Q4) how individual components and configuration choices affect the throughput–fidelity trade-off (§cite22†6.5 ); and (Q5) how GPU-call forwarding affects measured kernel duration (§cite23†6.6 ).\nL184: ### 6.1. Experimental Setup\nL185: \nL186: Testbed.  We evaluate KREX on two testbed platforms each containing two 8-GPU nodes, for a total of 16 NVIDIA H20 and 16 AMD MI308X GPUs.\nL187: Workloads.  We evaluate KREX by replaying more than 20k benchmarking commands (16k on NVIDIA and 4k on AMD) collected from real kernel-agent runs. To collect these traces, we integrate KREX with Atrex Kernel Agent (cite41†Yang et al., 2026 ; cite42†Alibaba Group, 2026a ) and kernel-design-agents (cite57†NVlabs, 2026 ) on NVIDIA, and GEAK (cite58†AMD-AGI, 2025 ) on AMD.\nL188: We run these agents on tasks from three kernel benchmarks: KernelBench (KB) (cite46†Ouyang et al., 2025 ), Atrex-Bench (AB) (cite41†Yang et al., 2026 ; cite59†Alibaba Group, 2026b ), and FlashInfer-Trace (FT) (cite60†FlashInfer Team, 2026 ), and record every benchmarking command together with its associated source code for replay.\nL189: Baselines.  We compare four baselines. The native baseline provides command-granular exclusivity by running one command per GPU without KREX. KREX-serial runs one command per GPU through KREX to assess the runtime’s effect without concurrent sharing. The full KREX configuration runs concurrent commands with region-granular exclusivity. The ungated configuration runs concurrent commands without region protection to evaluate throughput and measurement fidelity under unprotected sharing.\nL190: We define command concurrency as the number of concurrent commands per GPU. Unless otherwise specified, KREX and ungated use a command concurrency of 16 on H20 and 4 on MI308X, with MPS (cite44†NVIDIA Corporation, 2024a ) enabled for KREX on H20.\nL191: ### 6.2. Workload Characterization\nL192: \nL193: Figure 6. Distributions of workloads. (a) Command durations on two GPU models (log x-axis); (b) critical-region percentages of commands sampled on H20.\nL194: Figure cite61†6 a shows the distribution of command durations in the recorded agent sessions, with medians of 10.2 s on NVIDIA and 17.7 s on AMD. These durations reflect how long each command reserves the GPU under command-granular exclusivity. To quantify the fraction of execution time spent in critical regions, we also analyze 1069 commands from one hour of KREX-serial execution on H20. For each command, we sum the time spent in all of its critical regions.\nL195: Figure cite61†6 b shows that the median region fraction is only $12\\%$, and $73\\%$ of commands spend less than $25\\%$ of their execution time in regions. Median fractions vary across suites but remain below $25\\%$: $4.4\\%$ for KernelBench, $18.2\\%$ for FlashInfer-Trace, and $22.7\\%$ for Atrex-Bench. These results indicate substantial off-region work that KREX can overlap across commands.\nL196: ### 6.3. Throughput\nL197: \nL198: Figure 7. Benchmarking throughput.\nL199: \nL200: To measure benchmarking capacity, we conduct one-hour saturated replays with all baselines receiving the same command stream within each platform. This replay excludes variability from agent reasoning and LLM response latency, allowing us to measure the benchmarking system’s throughput in isolation. We report throughput per 8-GPU node.\nL260:   * Alibaba Group (2026b) Alibaba Group Atrex-bench: end-to-end operator generation benchmark for multiple DSLs. Note: cite106†https://github.com/alibaba/atrex-bench†github.com Cited by: cite105†§6.1 .\nL261:   * AMD-AGI (2025) AMD-AGI GEAK: generating efficient AI-centric kernels. Note: cite107†https://github.com/AMD-AGI/GEAK†github.com Cited by: cite105†§6.1 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXtrace: High-Fidelity GPU Intra-Kernel Tracing via Binary-Level Instruction Splicing (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.28769v1)\nciteturn84view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.28769v1\",\"pattern\":\"AMD\"}); Total lines: 541\nL337: ## 6. Conclusion\nL338: \nL339: Xtrace is a high-fidelity GPU kernel tracing system. It minimizes compile-time interference and runtime overhead by splicing the probe instructions directly into the compiled GPU kernel binary. On production LLM kernels, Xtrace preserves 94–98% of the original instructions and adds only 0.9–2.8% overhead. Its traces guide a coding agent to the true bottleneck of a kernel, and its binary-level splicing extends such tracing to closed-source vendor kernels.\nL340: ## References\nL341:   * [2] 2025. Nanotrace. cite144†https://github.com/aikitoria/nanotrace†github.com .\nL342:   * [3] Osayamen Jonathan Aimuyo, Byungsoo Oh, and Rachee Singh. 2025. FlashMoE: Fast Distributed MoE in a Single Kernel. CoRR abs/2506.04667 (2025). arXiv:2506.04667 cite145†https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.04667 L343:   * [4] AMD. 2023. “RDNA3” Instruction Set Architecture Reference Guide. cite146†https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/radeon-tech-docs/instruction-set-architectures/rdna3-shader-instruction-set-architecture-feb-2023_0.pdf†www.amd.com . Version dated 15 August 2023.\nL344:   * [5] AMD. 2025a. “AMD Instinct MI300” Instruction Set Architecture Reference Guide. cite147†https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/instinct-tech-docs/instruction-set-architectures/amd-instinct-mi300-cdna3-instruction-set-architecture.pdf†www.amd.com . Version dated 5 August 2025.\nL345:   * [6] AMD. 2025b. Composable Kernel. cite148†https://github.com/ROCm/composable_kernel†github.com .\nL346:   * [7] AMD. 2025c. “RDNA4” Instruction Set Architecture Reference Guide. cite149†https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/radeon-tech-docs/instruction-set-architectures/rdna4-instruction-set-architecture.pdf†www.amd.com . Version dated 7 April 2025.\nL347:   * [8] AMD. 2026a. FlyDSL. cite150†https://github.com/rocm/flydsl†github.com .\nL348:   * [9] AMD. 2026b. HIP Documentation. cite151†https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/HIP/en/latest/†rocm.docs.amd.com .\nL349:   * [10] AMD. 2026c. HIP Documentation: Debugging with HIP. cite152†https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/HIP/en/docs-6.3.2/how-to/debugging.html†rocm.docs.amd.com .\nL350:   * [11] AMD. 2026d. ROCgdb. cite153†https://github.com/ROCm/ROCgdb†github.com .\nL351:   * [12] AMD. 2026e. ROCprofiler-SDK documentation. cite154†https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/rocprofiler-sdk/en/latest/†rocm.docs.amd.com .\nL352:   * [13] David G. Bradlee, Susan J. Eggers, and Robert R. Henry. 1991. Integrating Register Allocation and Instruction Scheduling for RISCs. In ASPLOS-IV Proceedings - Forth International Conference on Architectural Support for Programming Languages and Operating Systems, Santa Clara, California, USA, April 8-11, 1991, David A. Patterson and Bob Rau (Eds.). ACM Press, 122–131. cite155†doi:10.1145/106972.106986†doi.org L353:   * [14] Graham R. Brightwell and Peter Winkler. 1991. Counting Linear Extensions is #P-Complete. In Proceedings of the 23rd Annual ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing, May 5-8, 1991, New Orleans, Louisiana, USA, Cris Koutsougeras and Jeffrey Scott Vitter (Eds.). ACM, 175–181. cite156†doi:10.1145/103418.103441†doi.org L354:   * [15] Gregory J. Chaitin. 1982. Register Allocation & Spilling via Graph Coloring. In Proceedings of the SIGPLAN ’82 Symposium on Compiler Construction, Boston, Massachusetts, USA, June 23-25, 1982, John R. White and Frances E. Allen (Eds.). ACM, 98–105. cite157†doi:10.1145/800230.806984†doi.org L355:   * [16] Chang Chen, Xiuhong Li, Qianchao Zhu, Jiangfei Duan, Peng Sun, Xingcheng Zhang, and Chao Yang. 2024. Centauri: Enabling Efficient Scheduling for Communication-Computation Overlap in Large Model Training via Communication Partitioning. In Proceedings of the 29th ACM International Conference on Architectural Support for Programming Languages and Operating Systems, Volume 3, ASPLOS 2024, La Jolla, CA, USA, 27 April 2024- 1 May 2024, Rajiv Gupta, Nael B.\nL356: Abu-Ghazaleh, Madan Musuvathi, and Dan Tsafrir (Eds.). ACM, 178–191. cite158†doi:10.1145/3620666.3651379†doi.org L357:   * [17] Sharan Chetlur, Cliff Woolley, Philippe Vandermersch, Jonathan Cohen, John Tran, Bryan Catanzaro, and Evan Shelhamer. 2014. cuDNN: Efficient Primitives for Deep Learning. CoRR abs/1410.0759 (2014). arXiv:1410.0759 cite159†http://arxiv.org/abs/1410.0759 L358:   * [18] Patrick H. Coppock, Brian Zhang, Eliot H. Solomon, Vasilis Kypriotis, Leon Yang, Bikash Sharma, Dan Schatzberg, Todd C. Mowry, and Dimitrios Skarlatos. 2025. LithOS: An Operating System for Efficient Machine Learning on GPUs. In Proceedings of the ACM SIGOPS 31st Symposium on Operating Systems Principles, SOSP 2025, Lotte Hotel World, Seoul, Republic of Korea, October 13-16, 2025, Youjip Won, Youngjin Kwon, Ding Yuan, and Rebecca Isaacs (Eds.). ACM, 1–17. cite160†doi:10.1145/3731569.3764818†doi.org L489: Its 32 ns granularity also misses short spans. Moreover, the global clock counts from GPU power-on, so its low 32 bits wrap around easily and a safe capture must take all 64 bits, costing two registers. Xtrace instead records the per-SM clock. The clock reads in tens of cycles, around 10 ns, and ticks once per cycle, so it resolves the short spans the global clock misses.\nL490: The clock also counts from kernel start, and a 32-bit capture wraps only after 1.4 to 3 seconds, longer than most kernels run, so one register suffices. However, per-SM clocks are mutually unsynchronized, so traces from two SMs cannot be aligned. Xtrace therefore calibrates them, reading the global and the per-SM clock back to back at each block’s start and end, which maps the SM’s cycles onto the global nanosecond timeline.\nL491: Recording the per-SM clock thus spares registers and removes the read latency from every RECORD probe.\nL492: #### A.2.2. AMD GPUs.\nL493: Reduced execution model.  AMD GPUs resolve most hazards in hardware, so Xtrace instantiates a reduced form of the execution model of §cite13†4.1 . A wave still issues its instructions in order, yet an AMD instruction carries no stall count and no scoreboard field. The hardware interlocks most register dependencies between arithmetic instructions, and the compiler pads only the few hazards it leaves unchecked with wait states [cite224†5 ]. A CDNA GPU expresses these wait states as s_nop instructions.\nL494: An RDNA3 or later GPU adds s_delay_alu, an optional hint that spaces two dependent arithmetic instructions and is not required for correctness [cite225†4 ]. Xtrace pads the probe with the same rules the compiler applies [cite109†44 ]. Every instruction between a hazard pair counts toward its wait states, as in the stall masking of §cite16†4.4 , so Xtrace adds only the padding the remainder requires.\nL495: Splicing only lengthens the distance between kernel instructions, so the kernel’s own wait states stay satisfied, and Xtrace keeps them unchanged.\nL496: Registers across VGPR, SGPR, and AGPR.  Xtrace runs the register liveness analysis of §cite15†4.3 over the three register classes an AMD GPU exposes [cite224†5 ]. A kernel keeps per-lane values in vector registers (VGPR) and per-wave values in scalar registers (SGPR), and a CDNA GPU adds accumulation registers (AGPR) that its matrix instructions read and write.\nL497: AMD GPUs carry no predicate register class, and predication instead rides the EXEC mask and VCC that the scalar registers back, so the probe draws its predicate state from the same SGPR pool. A matrix instruction holds its accumulation registers until it retires, so Xtrace keeps those registers live across its execution, as it does for the asynchronous operations of §cite15†4.3 .\nL498: When dead registers run short, Xtrace applies the two remedies of §cite15†4.3 on the kernel object that the driver reads to schedule the launch. It rewrites the granulated register count in the kernel descriptor, or it downgrades the probe to the per-wave SGPR s [cite109†44 ]. A CDNA GPU fixes the split between vector and accumulation registers at compile time through ACCUM_OFFSET, and its waves carry no instruction that reallocates the register budget at runtime.\nL499: An RDNA4 GPU instead reallocates that budget at runtime through s_alloc_vgpr, so Xtrace splices the INIT probe after the reallocation to give it a valid budget (§cite17†4.5 ).\nL500: Counter  | Outstanding operations it counts  | Bits\nL501: CDNA (gfx9)\nL502: vmcnt  | VMEM load, store, atomic  | 6\nL503: lgkmcnt  | LDS, GDS, scalar memory, message  | 4\nL504: expcnt  | export  | 3\nL505: RDNA3 (gfx11)\nL506: vmcnt  | VMEM load  | 6\nL507: vscnt  | VMEM store  | 6\nL508: lgkmcnt  | LDS, scalar memory, message  | 6\nL509: expcnt  | export  | 3\nL510: RDNA4 (gfx12)\nL511: loadcnt  | VMEM load  | 6\nL512: storecnt  | VMEM store  | 6\nL513: dscnt  | LDS  | 6\nL514: kmcnt  | scalar memory, message  | 5\nL515: samplecnt  | texture sample  | 6\nL516: bvhcnt  | ray-tracing traversal  | 3\nL517: expcnt  | export  | 3\nL518: Table 5. The per-wave completion counters an AMD GPU tracks, by architecture, and the bit width of each [cite224†5 , cite225†4 , cite226†7 ]. VMEM, LDS, and GDS denote vector memory, local data share, and global data share operations. A wave waits on a counter with s_waitcnt, or with s_wait_* on gfx12. A probe on a compute kernel enters only the memory counters, so samplecnt and bvhcnt never concern it.\nL519: Counters in place of scoreboards.  AMD GPUs track variable-latency operations with per-wave counters rather than named scoreboards, so a spliced memory operation enters the counters that the kernel’s waits read. The set of counters differs across AMD architectures, which Table cite227†5 lists.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDream-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through Evolving Worlds (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14858v2)\nciteturn84view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14858v2\",\"pattern\":\"L40S\"}); Total lines: 3011\nL300: We take responsibility for the final content of this work, including text, claims or artifacts produced with the aid of generative AI.\nL301: ## Appendix B Detailed Experimental Settings\nL302: \nL303: ### B.1 Evaluation Environments\nL304: \nL305: #### Lasso regularization-path discovery.\nL306: \nL307: We evaluate candidate solvers in a KVM virtual machine with an AMD EPYC 7B12 processor, 8 vCPUs, and 32 GB RAM, running Debian-based Linux. The evaluation protocol follows cite64†Ye et al. (2026) .\nL308: #### GPU kernel engineering.\nL309: \nL310: We evaluate candidate kernels on an NVIDIA L40S GPU. Numerical correctness is checked against the reference PyTorch implementation following the official KernelBench protocol (cite87†Ouyang et al., 2025 ).\nL311: \nL312: #### Mathematical optimization.\nL313: \nL314: We use the same hardware environment as for Lasso regularization-path discovery.\nL315: #### Harness optimization.\nL316: \nL317: We evaluate candidate harnesses on a CPU server with 96 physical cores (192 logical CPUs) and 708 GiB RAM, using Docker to provide isolated task execution environments.\nL318: ### B.2 Discovery Configuration\nL319: \nL320: Table cite127†3 summarizes the discovery configurations. Each offline phase evaluates the current policy and three newly generated policy versions ($M=4$). The parenthesized budget decomposition specifies the maximum number of branches and attempts per branch, including the initial attempt. These budgets are upper limits; actual discovery-agent calls depend on the policy’s allocation and stopping decisions.\nL321: Table 3: Discovery configurations. $T$ denotes the number of online discovery rounds, $W$ the maximum number of parallel workers, $B$ the maximum discovery-agent calls per online round, and $M$ the number of candidate policies evaluated per offline phase, including the current policy.\nL322: Task  | Discovery Model  | $\\boldsymbol{T}$  | $\\boldsymbol{W}$  | $\\boldsymbol{B}$  | $\\boldsymbol{M}$\nL323: Lasso  | Gemini-3.1-Pro  | 5  | 10  | $110\\;(10\\times 11)$  | 4\nL324: Lasso  | Gemini-3.7-Flash  | 5  | 32  | $640\\;(32\\times 20)$  | 4\nL325: VGG16  | Gemini-3.1-Pro  | 9  | 10  | $110\\;(10\\times 11)$  | 4\nL326: LayerNorm  | Gemini-3.1-Pro  | 9  | 10  | $110\\;(10\\times 11)$  | 4\nL327: ConvDiv  | Gemini-3.1-Pro  | 9  | 10  | $110\\;(10\\times 11)$  | 4\nL328: ConvMax  | Gemini-3.1-Pro  | 9  | 10  | $110\\;(10\\times 11)$  | 4\n"
